I reproduced a cleanup failure on AX `ac2332829f22360ff97b0ba34d94dd0dd782f17e` and the compatibility branch `be7b1a8dc75c7307193f28ec932fe582ec6a0aed`. Substrate was `269df772aa22e616719b1844dde8ecb6c5e79947`. The cleanup code is unchanged by the AX compatibility fix.

Upstream checked 2026-10-04: AX main remains `ac23328`, and the compatibility fork still points to `be7b1a8`. Neither this cleanup fix nor the compatibility fix is merged upstream. The repository API confirms collaborators-only PR creation, so this issue offers a tested patch for maintainers to review.

AX already retries `DeleteActorTemplate` five times, 500 ms apart. One `Aborted` is handled. The leak occurs when Substrate's ActorTemplate lease remains busy beyond that approximately 2.5-second window. The golden-snapshot reconciler holds the lease across boot, snapshot, and tagging; the original lab evidence puts one lease window at at least six seconds.

When retries fail, `ReconcileDelete` logs the template cleanup error and returns nil. `Server.DeleteTask` then removes the task record and reports success. A later delete returns `NotFound`; no background sweep retries the orphan template. This can retain the associated golden snapshot. The original fake-service regression reproduces the failure on both AX revisions with a three-second lease. This establishes the failure mechanism, not its production frequency or a Substrate-version regression.

My local patch gives listing and deleting all matching templates one shared 60-second context budget, capped by the caller's earlier deadline. `Aborted` retries use 500 ms exponential backoff capped at five seconds. Other errors return to the caller; cancellation stops the retry loop. Existing client normalization treats `NotFound` as completed deletion.

Cleanup failure now propagates from `ReconcileDelete`. The existing server then keeps the task in `Terminating`, so a subsequent delete can finish cleanup. Success requires completing the cleanup represented by the list result before removing the task record.

Tests exercise a lease longer than the old window, permanent failure followed by recovery, list failure, already-deleted resources, blocked RPCs, shorter caller deadlines, and cancellation followed by a fresh delete. The full AX suite passes with the race detector on both the upstream-base patch and the compatibility base. Three deletion samples on a fresh two-worker cluster leave zero matching templates; one samples a golden actor in RESUMING and completes in 2.015 seconds. These small samples support integration behavior, not a production failure-rate estimate.

Related: [AX #360](https://github.com/google/ax/issues/360) reports leaked templates from later list pages. Pagination is a separate trigger and remains outside this patch. [AX #433](https://github.com/google/ax/issues/433) discusses changing ActorTemplate use. This draft does not propose a broad orphan collector, which would require ownership and concurrency rules.

The patch below targets current AX main `ac23328` and is independent of the compatibility update. Run the complete suite with `go test -race ./... -count=1`. The new `internal/server/template_cleanup_test.go` exercises the real AX gRPC server, reconciler and Substrate client with an in-process fake Substrate service. AI assisted with implementation, testing, and this report.

